WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.290
- We have a verdict

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.240
in the Johnny Depp-Amber Heard case.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.290
And like everything in the trial itself,

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:06.230
it's controversial.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:07.940
The jury ruled in favor of Johnny Depp

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.010
in his libel suit against Amber Heard.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.260
Depp sued his former wife

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.590
for writing about being
a domestic abuse victim

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.500
because he said he never abused her.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.177
The jury awarded Depp
$15 million in damages,

00:00:18.177 --> 00:00:19.960
$10 million in compensatory damages

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.158
and $5 million in punitive damages.

00:00:22.158 --> 00:00:24.510
The jury also ruled in
favor of Amber Heard

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.550
on one of her counterclaims against Depp.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.770
She alleged that Depp directed

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:28.840
a smear campaign against her,

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.070
having his ex-lawyer saying

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:32.910
she fabricated the evidence of her abuse.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.180
The jury awarded her $2 million

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.540
in compensatory damages

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.900
and nothing in punitives.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.481
So, at the end of the day, they both win!

00:00:39.481 --> 00:00:41.040
Hooray!

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:41.873
No, but seriously,

00:00:41.873 --> 00:00:44.180
how can they both have defamed each other?

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.779
Let's dig into this verdict.

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:48.490
(light music)

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:49.350
Now, first of all,

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.690
and I hate that I have to say this,

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.080
no one is going to jail.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.210
This is a civil case and no one is guilty.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.660
People are liable in a civil case.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.930
They are not guilty or not guilty,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.783
despite what some punitive
law professor has said.

00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:05.300
This is a civil trial.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.730
There's no guilt involved.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:07.563
Now, in fairness,

00:01:07.563 --> 00:01:08.396
about half the states

00:01:08.396 --> 00:01:10.520
do have a criminal defamation statute,

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:11.727
including Virginia,

00:01:11.727 --> 00:01:14.040
but this is not one of those cases.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.050
It's just two people suing each other.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.280
The state is not involved.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.630
But as you probably know,

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:19.960
here we have two public figures

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.400
suing each other for defamation.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:22.950
And when you have celebrities,

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.516
you generally have a
higher standard of proof

00:01:24.516 --> 00:01:25.800
to make your claim.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.520
The law makes it difficult
for public people

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:28.980
to win a defamation suit,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.690
even if a published story
contains inaccuracies.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.070
In order for a public figure to win,

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:34.640
the jury has to find that the defendant

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.000
made statements about
them with actual malice.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.790
Actual malice means
knowledge of the falsity,

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:41.623
or at a minimum,

00:01:41.623 --> 00:01:44.380
a reckless disregard for
the truth of a statement.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.160
It has nothing to do with animosity.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.170
Now, as you probably know,

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.650
Amber Heard wrote an op-ed
in The Washington Post

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.377
that contained these sentences, quote,

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:52.227
"Then two years ago,

00:01:52.227 --> 00:01:54.587
"I became a public figure
representing domestic abuse,

00:01:54.587 --> 00:01:57.287
"and I felt the full force
of our culture's wrath

00:01:57.287 --> 00:01:58.520
"for women who speak out."

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.787
And, quote, "I had the rare vantage point

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:01.777
"of seeing in real time,

00:02:01.777 --> 00:02:04.490
"how institutions protect
men accused of abuse."

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.590
Now, Johnny Depp
challenged these sentences

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:07.950
and also the headline written

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.353
by The Washington Post itself, quote,

00:02:09.353 --> 00:02:12.187
"Amber Heard: I spoke
up about sexual violence

00:02:12.187 --> 00:02:13.747
"and faced our culture's wrath.

00:02:13.747 --> 00:02:15.360
"That has to change."

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.193
Depp sued Heard,

00:02:16.193 --> 00:02:17.760
alleging these sentences were defamatory.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.000
And during closing arguments,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.200
Heard's lawyer told the jury

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.440
that if they believed Depp abused her,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:22.800
even one time,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.670
that he cannot prove defamation.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.180
- [Lawyer] If Amber
was abused by Mr. Depp,

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.010
even one time, then she wins.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:30.917
- Now, during deliberations,

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:32.130
the jury had to evaluate

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.120
the evidence that they heard

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.710
over the six-week trial,

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.160
and apply the jury instructions

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.690
given to them by the judge.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:39.160
With Depp's defamation claims,

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:40.690
they were looking at Instruction C,

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:41.820
which you can see here.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.040
So, the jury had to decide

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:43.900
whether Heard made

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.635
or republished any of these statements,

00:02:45.635 --> 00:02:47.330
these were false statements,

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.720
whether they harmed his reputation,

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.510
and whether Amber Heard
acted with actual malice.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:52.343
They answered, "Yes."

00:02:52.343 --> 00:02:54.160
Yes, she said or republished these words.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:55.070
Yes, they were false.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.720
And yes, they hurt his reputation.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.710
The jury likely decided
everything that Amber Heard

00:02:58.710 --> 00:02:59.880
said about abuse was false,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:00.713
and that she did it

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:02.230
with reckless disregard for the truth.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.063
And as a result,

00:03:03.063 --> 00:03:03.896
Johnny Depp sustained

00:03:03.896 --> 00:03:05.577
about $15 million in damages,

00:03:05.577 --> 00:03:07.610
$10 million in compensatory damages,

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:09.280
which is meant to compensate him

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.100
for the damage that he actually suffered,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.320
as well as $5 million in punitives

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.070
that is to punish Amber
Heard for her statements.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.530
Now, the judge cut the
punitives down to $350,000,

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.200
bringing the total to $10.35 million.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.670
Now those are obviously large numbers

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.790
but it kind of makes sense

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.063
in the context of Johnny Depp.

00:03:25.063 --> 00:03:28.224
He is a famous movie star and he alleges,

00:03:28.224 --> 00:03:29.960
probably truthfully,

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.760
that these allegations
have really hurt his career

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.120
and hurt his ability to make

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.370
millions and millions of dollars.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.180
So, it's entirely possible
that he lost $10 million

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:39.180
as a result of the statements

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.430
that Amber Heard made against him.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.900
And given the general nature
of Heard's statements,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.650
the fact that the jury
found in Depp's favor

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:46.788
probably means that the
jury likely didn't believe

00:03:46.788 --> 00:03:50.260
any of Heard's substantive
claims of abuse.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.110
Now, some have argued
that Heard got a raw deal

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.730
because her column did
not mention Depp by name,

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:56.850
but Depp successfully argued
that it was an example

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.540
of what's called
defamation by implication,

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:00.290
because parts of the column referred

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:01.320
to the allegations of abuse

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.210
that she made against him

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.060
following their breakup in 2016.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:04.893
And it's probably fair

00:04:04.893 --> 00:04:05.830
to say that the general public

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.620
knew about Heard's connection to Depp,

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:09.225
and that they would've assumed

00:04:09.225 --> 00:04:11.265
that the allegations made in the op-ed,

00:04:11.265 --> 00:04:14.290
referred to Depp because
of that connection

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:16.443
and that she was
directing those statements

00:04:16.443 --> 00:04:17.840
at Johnny Depp.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.020
And there's plenty of law on this.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.030
This is not unusual

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:21.927
for a defamation by implication case.

00:04:21.927 --> 00:04:23.810
And that takes us to Amber
Heard's counterclaims.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.200
You'd think that if Heard defamed Depp,

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.250
then they would dismiss Heard's claims

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:30.150
and that would be the end of the story.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.290
But what fun would that be?

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:33.123
As you know,

00:04:33.123 --> 00:04:35.790
the jury also found
that Depp defamed Heard,

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.448
but it looks like that
relates to a statement

00:04:37.448 --> 00:04:39.020
only tangentially related

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:40.750
to the main abuse statements.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.970
Now, after Depp sued Heard in 2021,

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:44.750
she filed a counterclaim against him.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.710
She said that Depp directed
his ex-lawyer, Adam Waldman,

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:49.410
to make defamatory statements about her

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:50.500
including these three.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:51.457
Statement A, quote,

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:53.267
"Amber Heard and her friends in the media

00:04:53.267 --> 00:04:54.957
"used fake sexual violence allegations

00:04:54.957 --> 00:04:56.037
"as both a sword and a shield,

00:04:56.037 --> 00:04:57.717
"depending on their needs.

00:04:57.717 --> 00:05:00.387
"They have selected some
of her sexual violence

00:05:00.387 --> 00:05:01.667
"hoax 'facts' as the sword,

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:04.080
"inflicting them on the
public and Mr. Depp."

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.127
Statement B, quote,

00:05:05.127 --> 00:05:06.867
"Quite simply this was an ambush, a hoax.

00:05:06.867 --> 00:05:09.027
"They set Mr. Depp up by calling the cops

00:05:09.027 --> 00:05:10.777
"but the first step didn't do the trick.

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:12.327
"The officers came to the penthouses,

00:05:12.327 --> 00:05:13.487
"thoroughly searched and interviewed,

00:05:13.487 --> 00:05:16.607
"and left after seeing no
damage to face or property.

00:05:16.607 --> 00:05:18.817
"So Amber and her friends
spilled a little wine

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:19.857
"and roughed up the place,

00:05:19.857 --> 00:05:21.067
"got their stories straight

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:23.147
"under the direction of
a lawyer and publicist,

00:05:23.147 --> 00:05:25.720
"and then placed a second call to 911."

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:26.553
Statement C, quote,

00:05:26.553 --> 00:05:28.717
"We have reached the beginning of the end

00:05:28.717 --> 00:05:31.440
"of Ms. Heard's abuse
hoax against Mr. Depp."

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:32.640
And the jury reviewed the evidence,

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:33.473
and with respect

00:05:33.473 --> 00:05:34.480
to at least one of these statements.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:35.910
believed that that was the case.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.100
Specifically, they
decided that Statement B,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.100
the long paragraph about

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:41.150
a specific incidence, was false.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:42.740
The main facts about that incident

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:43.650
were relayed to the jury

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:44.677
by Heard and her friends,

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.500
and the very same people
that the jury found

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.300
had completely made up
things in order to hurt Depp.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:50.170
Now, we'll need to hear

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:51.150
from the jury to understand

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.130
exactly what they were thinking,

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:53.440
but we can presume that the jury

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.273
reviewed the evidence

00:05:54.273 --> 00:05:55.697
and concluded that Amber
Heard and her friends

00:05:55.697 --> 00:05:58.860
didn't orchestrate an abuse
hoax on that occasion.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:01.190
Maybe they didn't spill
wine or rough up the place.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:03.430
They didn't work with
a lawyer or publicist.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.080
They might not have gotten
their stories straight.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:07.530
If this is what the jury meant,

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.510
then they could be claiming

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:09.470
that the underlying incident

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:10.560
is still based on a lie,

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.080
but that Heard orchestrated
the lie in a different way,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.320
or that she was lying about other things

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:16.340
and not this thing.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:17.480
But at the end of the day,

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:19.400
the jury awarded $2 million to Heard

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.230
for this particular falsity.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:22.510
These same jurors probably found

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:24.780
that she acted with actual
malice in concocting a hoax

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:26.440
that included fake bruises

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:28.080
and fake tears and fake claims.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.170
And thus, statements A and C

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:31.830
were therefore not defamatory,

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:32.690
only Statement B,

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:33.920
which referred to a very

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:36.120
specific tangential incident.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:37.300
So both Depp and Heard

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.170
were awarded compensatory damages.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:40.250
These are things that are meant

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:41.181
to compensate a plaintiff

00:06:41.181 --> 00:06:42.490
to be in the same position

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:43.323
that they would be

00:06:43.323 --> 00:06:44.510
if they weren't injured.

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:46.040
Things like financial losses,

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:47.510
medical bills, lost income,

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:49.140
and reduced earning potential.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:50.650
And a damage award can also consider

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:52.110
non-monetary losses as well,

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.180
like emotional pain or physical pain,

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:55.210
loss of companionship,

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:57.540
all of those would be
considered compensatory damages.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.510
But Johnny Depp was awarded
punitive damages as well,

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:01.990
which are designed to warn others

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:03.902
and punish this particular defendant.

00:07:03.902 --> 00:07:05.280
They are assessed when a jury

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:06.130
wants to punish someone

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:07.050
who acted totally outside

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.550
the bounds of appropriate conduct.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:10.280
And they're only available in Virginia

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.310
if the defendant has acted
wantonly, oppressively,

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:13.143
or with such malice

00:07:13.143 --> 00:07:15.210
as to evince a spirit of malice

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:18.090
or criminal indifference
to civil obligations.

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:19.360
But in Virginia,

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:23.890
punitive damages are capped
at $350,000 by statute.

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:25.240
Now, the jury found that Amber Heard

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:26.087
had defamed Johnny Depp

00:07:26.087 --> 00:07:27.840
and that her conduct satisfied

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.070
the very high standard

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:30.620
for punitive damages.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.217
Now they awarded him a lot of money,

00:07:32.217 --> 00:07:34.150
$5 million in punitive damages,

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:35.200
but the judge reduced

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:38.240
that damages award to $350,000,

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:39.620
as required by statute.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:42.180
Now, the jury wouldn't have known that

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:43.670
going into deliberations.

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:46.090
That's to make sure that
they don't shift all

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:47.680
of the punitive damages

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:50.240
into the compensatory damages award.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:51.980
But after the verdict was reached,

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.700
the judge capped the amount
that was required by law.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:56.627
Tangentially, you might
also hear lawyers referring

00:07:56.627 --> 00:07:58.590
to the State Farm standard,

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.130
where punitive damages are capped

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:02.661
as a matter of constitutional law.

00:08:02.661 --> 00:08:05.030
That's a Supreme Court case.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:06.030
That doesn't apply here.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:08.250
There was a specific statute in Virginia

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:11.280
that limited punitive damages to $350,000.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:12.770
That doesn't really implicate

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:14.880
the State Farm Supreme Court case.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:15.713
That being said,

00:08:15.713 --> 00:08:17.720
after the punitive damages were capped,

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:21.720
Johnny Depp was entitled
to $10.35 million,

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:23.782
though Amber Heard was awarded $2 million

00:08:23.782 --> 00:08:25.500
in compensatory damages,

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:26.870
related to the Statement B

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:28.480
that we just talked about,

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:31.839
netting Johnny Depp $8.35 million

00:08:31.839 --> 00:08:33.060
at the end of the day.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:34.230
So, what's next?

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:36.300
Can Johnny Depp sue The Washington Post?

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:39.120
Well, sure, but it's incredibly
unlikely that he'd win.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.520
You can sue anyone for
anything at anytime.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:42.360
But there's no reason

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:43.300
to believe that The Washington Post

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:46.190
would've known of the
falsity of the statements.

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:47.023
And on top of that,

00:08:47.023 --> 00:08:48.270
The Washington Post simply

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:49.827
published Amber Heard's article.

00:08:49.827 --> 00:08:51.700
The only part of the
article that was written

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.507
by The Washington Post, was the headline,

00:08:53.507 --> 00:08:55.767
"Amber Heard: I spoke
up about sexual violence

00:08:55.767 --> 00:08:56.957
"and faced our culture's wrath.

00:08:56.957 --> 00:08:57.810
"That has to change."

00:08:57.810 --> 00:08:59.900
Which more or less summarizes the things

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:01.640
that Amber Heard actually said.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:02.800
So good luck proving knowledge

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:03.837
of falsity or recklessness

00:09:03.837 --> 00:09:04.928
with respect to the truth

00:09:04.928 --> 00:09:07.050
by The Washington Post itself.

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:08.930
Though given that the
article is still online,

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:10.380
Depp might sue for an injunction

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:11.660
to take that article down.

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:12.740
But by that same token,

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:15.238
could Amber Heard sue the ACLU?

00:09:15.238 --> 00:09:17.590
The Washington Post
published Heard's op-ed

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:19.850
but the ACLU helped her write it.

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:21.700
Heard had promised to donate $7 million

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:23.860
from her divorce settlement
to two charities,

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:24.840
including the ACLU.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.600
And so far, she's only paid

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:28.430
the ACLU part of that money.

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:30.640
Could she claim that they did a bad job

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.239
of ghostwriting the
op-ed, maybe negligence?

00:09:33.239 --> 00:09:36.070
Well again, you can sue anyone
for anything at anytime.

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:37.470
Sure, this is theoretically possible,

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:38.940
but it would be extremely complicated

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:40.710
and probably doomed,

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:42.700
though more tenuous
arguments have been made.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.770
Maybe Heard will use
it to try and claw back

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:46.280
some of her donation, who knows?

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:47.220
Complete speculation.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:50.500
But what about an appeal
of the jury's verdict?

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:52.220
Amber Heard has vowed to appeal.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:53.910
And even though Depp was also found

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:54.743
to have defamed Heard,

00:09:54.743 --> 00:09:56.147
Depp was not surprisingly,

00:09:56.147 --> 00:09:58.120
very pleased with the verdict.

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.420
Depp said that Heard's
op-ed changed his whole life

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:01.253
in the blink of an eye,

00:10:01.253 --> 00:10:03.920
but the jury had given his life back.

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:05.790
Now, Heard was not
pleased with the verdict

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:06.657
and said that she was

00:10:06.657 --> 00:10:08.005
"heartbroken that the mountain of evidence

00:10:08.005 --> 00:10:10.747
"still was not enough to stand
up to the disproportionate

00:10:10.747 --> 00:10:12.720
"power, influence, and
sway of my ex-husband."

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.020
And although people talk about

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:15.304
what precedent this verdict sets,

00:10:15.304 --> 00:10:17.380
what lawyers mean by
precedent is different

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:19.830
from how regular people
often use that word.

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:20.913
In the law, precedent is set

00:10:20.913 --> 00:10:22.863
by published judicial opinions.

00:10:22.863 --> 00:10:23.970
Legally speaking,

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:26.290
jury verdicts don't set
any precedent whatsoever.

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:28.502
You can't cite to the Amber Heard case

00:10:28.502 --> 00:10:32.140
and hope that some other jury or judge

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:32.973
will follow it.

00:10:32.973 --> 00:10:34.410
But if the case goes up on appeal

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:35.753
and there is a written opinion,

00:10:35.753 --> 00:10:38.550
that opinion would have
precedential effect,

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:40.420
which is one reason, potentially,

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:42.060
the parties might settle before

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.220
the appeal is completed.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:46.700
They might just do it to
get rid of uncertainty

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:49.730
with what the Court of
Appeal might or might not do.

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:51.760
But no doubt, when this
case goes up on appeal,

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:52.593
